<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burao</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burao is a city in the Togdheer region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, on the seasonal Togdheer river. It was a seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habr-yunis">Habr Yunis</a> sultans and a centre of the Isaaq clans, and in May 1991 it hosted the conference at which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-national-movement">Somali National Movement</a> declared the restoration of Somaliland's independence. It is now one of Somaliland's largest cities and a major livestock market.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burao.jpg" alt="Burao" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jethrostrong1 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burao,_Somaliland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> word burco describes open, elevated land with soft, rich soil, and the Burao area is considered fertile. The city began in the late 18th century as a well, called Ceel-Gooni, used by nomads of the surrounding area. A town grew around it, and because of the water along the dry valley it became a small inland market linking the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> with the interior and offering trading facilities to the region's nomadic population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For much of the 19th century Burao was the capital of the Habr Yunis Sultanate, ruled at different times by Sultan Nur Ahmed Aman, Sultan Awad Deria and Sultan Madar Hersi. The explorer Frank Linsly James, a guest of Sultan Awad Deria in 1884, described a display by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habr-yunis">Habr Yunis</a> horsemen in the bed of the Togdheer River. British forces burned the settlement to the ground in 1900, and the modern town was re-established in 1910. The Dervish movement was founded at Burao in 1899, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-abdullah-hassan">Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</a> and his followers assembled there with other clans and declared open hostility to the British.</p><p>In 1922 the people of Burao rose against a heavy tax. In the disturbances Captain Allan Gibb, the district commissioner, was shot dead, and aircraft of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a>, requested from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, bombed the town with incendiaries and burned the settlement to the ground; the British afterwards abandoned the tax policy. In July 1945 the religious leader Sheikh Bashir led a rebellion of Habr Je'lo tribesmen that began with an attack on Burao's prison; he was killed in the mountains of Bur Dhab, and a secondary school in Burao bears his name. In the 1950s the expanding livestock trade, with animals exported to Saudi Arabia, made Burao the second largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>.</p><p>In May 1988 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-national-movement">Somali National Movement</a> captured Burao in about two hours, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> army replied with heavy artillery, tanks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aerial-bombardment">aerial bombardment</a>. Reports describe killings of civilians, and the city was razed and most of its inhabitants fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. In May 1991 a Central Committee of the movement met at Burao and agreed to revoke the union with the Somali Democratic Republic, forming the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdirahman-ahmed-ali-tuur">Abdirahman Ahmed Ali Tuur</a> elected to govern for two years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Burao is the capital of the Togdheer region and the second largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>. Its main residents are the Habr Je'lo, Habr Yonis, Isamusa and Arap, all subdivisions of the Isaaq clan-family. The city is divided into four wards: Mohamed Ali, Farah Omar, Lihle and Sheikh Bashir.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the SNM attacked <b>Burao</b> and Hargeisa in 1988, government forces bombarded the cities, destroying much of Hargeisa and killing many thousands of civilians, while hundreds of thousands of people fled to Ethiopia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its sultans were based near Toon and later in the area of <b>Burao</b> in northern Somalia.</blockquote><a href="/state/habr-yunis-sultanate">Habr Yunis Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
